lolcat: byte I/O instead of wide stdio

lolcat 1.4 reads with fgetwc, writes with putwchar and formats every colour
change with wprintf; on musl these are most of its time. The characters
are now read with read(2) into a buffer and decoded as UTF-8 here, written
as their own bytes through an output buffer flushed when full and before
waiting for input (so a partial line or tail -f shows at once), and the
256-colour escapes are made once. Printable ASCII skips wcwidth.

The output is the same byte for byte, with two exceptions: a malformed
UTF-8 sequence passes through instead of ending the input, and with
--invert non-ASCII text is no longer turned into "?" (the original
oriented stdout before setlocale). On the C906 (1 GHz): 1 MB of dmesg
3.13 s -> 0.21 s, 25 KB 104 -> 32 ms.

